  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce final answer detailed technical tutorial. Need include parsing, planning, execution, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows, concrete examples, tradeoffs, pitfalls. Must use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself. Need likely structured. Need be careful no repetition? User says avoid repeating yourself. We can be concise but detailed, no redundant restatements.\n\nNeed maybe mention MVCC, locking, isolation levels, buffer pool, optimizer, statistics, cost model, execution engine, access paths, join algorithms, indexes (B-tree, hash, covering, composite order, selectivity), query rewrites, prepared statements, caching, autocommit, deadlock, concurrency. Tradeoffs: index size vs read speed, write amplification, fragmentation, stats stale, optimizer wrong, join order, index-only vs table lookup, hash vs nested loop vs sort merge, materialized subqueries, temp tables, spilling to disk, lock contention, isolation read anomalies, deadlocks, buffer pool pollution, row-level vs page-level locks.\n\nNeed concrete examples SQL. Maybe show table definitions:\nemployees (id, dept_id, name, salary)\ndepartments (id, name, region)\ntransactions maybe.\n\nExplain lifecycle:\n1 Client sends SQL; protocol, parse: lexical, syntax, semantic checks, normalization.\n2 Preparse: parameterization, prepared statement caching.\n3 Optimization: expand views, subquery unnesting, predicate pushdown, join reordering, choose join order and algorithms, access paths via statistics (histograms, NDV, correlation), cost model CPU+IO, constraints, query hints.\n4 Physical plan: operators (scan, filter, join, aggregate, sort, limit, etc).\n5 Execution: buffer pool, page read, predicate evaluation, join operators, indexes, top-n, early termination, parallelism, spilling.\n6 Concurrency/transactions: transaction context, snapshot, locks/latches, isolation levels, MVCC, write-ahead log, commit/abort.\n7 Return rows: row format, cursor, streaming, batch, memory management.\n\nNeed examples. Let's craft with table and query.\nTables:\nCREATE TABLE employees (\n  id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n",
